"page": "Chapter 1: containers",
"title": "Running containers",
"url": "chapters/01-containers/index.html#running-containers",
"text": "These commands cover the lab. Each one has a page in the Docker CLI reference. Command What it does docker run -d --name <name> -p <host port>:<container port> <image> Starts a container in the background (-d), names it, and publishes a container port on a port of the VM docker ps Lists running containers. docker ps -a also lists stopped ones docker logs <name> Shows what the app wrote to its standard output and error. Add -f to follow docker exec -it <name> <command> Runs another command inside a running container docker stop <name>, docker rm <name> Stops, then removes a container docker image ls, docker image inspect <image> Lists images, shows the metadata of one image Publishing a port is what makes the app reachable. EXPOSE in the Dockerfile only documents the port, while -p 8080:8080 actually forwards port 8080 of the VM to port 8080 of the container. Containers log to standard output instead of files, and the engine collects those lines. Kubernetes does the same, so kubectl logs in chapter 2 works like docker logs. docker exec needs the command to exist in the image. In an image built from scratch there is no shell, so docker exec -it <name> sh fails. That is expected, and is one more reason why such images are hard to attack. the container runs, docker ps shows it, but curl http://localhost:8080 cannot connect. What do you check first? That the port is published: docker ps shows a PORTS column such as 0.0.0.0:8080->8080/tcp. Without -p, the app listens inside the con"
