"title": "Running as a non-root user",
"url": "chapters/01-containers/index.html#running-as-a-non-root-user",
"text": "Unless the image says otherwise, the process in a container runs as root (user ID 0). The kernel isolation limits what it can reach, but if an attacker breaks out of the container, being root makes everything worse. A USER instruction makes the app run as an ordinary user. Dockerfile USER Use a numeric user ID, such as 65532, rather than a name. Kubernetes can check at start-up that a container does not run as user 0 (runAsNonRoot, chapter 4). Security context That check needs a number it can compare: with a user name, the container is refused with the error \"image has non-numeric user\". Red Hat knowledge base article on this error OpenShift goes further: by default it runs each container with an arbitrary user ID chosen by the platform, in the root group (group ID 0), whatever the image says. Images must therefore work for any user ID, never need root, and listen on a port above 1023, because ports below 1024 need root. The sample app listens on 8080 and writes nothing to disk, so it already fits. Adapting containers to run on OpenShift (written in 2020, checked again in chapter 7) why can the sample app not listen on port 80 when it runs as user 65532? On Linux, binding to a port below 1024 needs root privileges, or a specific capability. A non-root process listens on a higher port, such as 8080, and the platform maps port 80 to it when needed, as -p 80:8080 would."
