"title": "Image size",
"url": "chapters/01-containers/index.html#image-size",
"text": "Every node that runs your app pulls the image first, and every byte in it is something to patch and scan. A small image starts faster and leaves less for an attacker to use. The main levers are a multi-stage build, a small base image, a .dockerignore file, and cleaning up in the same RUN instruction that created the files. Building best practices For a static Go binary, the final image can be a few megabytes. The validation script warns above 100 MB. you delete a large file in a RUN instruction placed after the COPY that added it. Does the image get smaller? No. The file stays in the layer created by COPY, and the later layer only records that it was deleted. Avoid copying it in the first place, or use a stage that is discarded."
