"text": "Pick an answer, then check it. The explanation opens below each question. What does EXPOSE 8080 in a Dockerfile do? Question 1 A. It publishes port 8080 of the container on the host. B. It opens port 8080 in the firewall of the VM. C. It records that the app listens on 8080, as documentation and metadata. D. It makes the app listen on port 8080. Answer and explanation Answer: C. It records that the app listens on 8080, as documentation and metadata. EXPOSE adds the port to the image metadata, where tools and people can read it. It does not publish the port on the host, it does not touch any firewall, and it does not change the port the app listens on. Publishing happens at run time with -p. See the Dockerfile reference. Which reference always points to the same image content? Question 2 A. sample-app:latest B. sample-app:1.0.0 C. sample-app@sha256:<digest> D. sample-app Answer and explanation Answer: C. sample-app@sha256:<digest>. A digest is computed from the content of the image, so it always designates the same bytes. Tags, including latest and 1.0.0, are labels that can be moved to another image. A name without a tag means latest. See Pull an image by digest. In a two-stage build, what ends up in the final image? Question 3 A. Everything from both stages. B. The base image of the last stage plus the files copied into it. C. Only the first stage, because it holds the compiler. D. The source code, so that the app can be rebuilt at start-up. Answer and explanation Answer: B."
